TRAINING LOG  |  wav2vec2 + LoRA  |  full training run on the server  |  FINISHED
====================================================================================================

MODEL
  base model    facebook/wav2vec2-xls-r-300m (pretrained speech model)
  fine-tuning   LoRA rank 32, alpha 64, dropout 0.05 in the attention (q, k, v, out) and feed-forward layers,
                plus a new CTC output layer for 35 tokens that is trained fully
  trainable     14,191,651 of 329,666,246 parameters (4.30%)
  code          src/train.py

DATA
  train        17,685 clips, 77.45 h = STT train (15,595 clips, 74.68 h) + HF train (2,090 clips, 2.77 h)
  validation   STT validation: 1,950 clips, 9.28 h
  test         STT test: 1,950 clips, 9.18 h   |   HF test: 523 clips, 0.71 h
  folders      STT = data/final/stt, HF = data/final/hf (Mendeley is not used)

RUN
  machine         server (NVIDIA GB10 GPU), 2026-10-14, finished 18:16
  settings        4 epochs = 2,212 steps, 32 clips per step (batch 8 x 4 accumulation), learning rate 5e-4, 10% warmup
  training time   9,059 s (2 h 31 min)
  saved to        outputs/wav2vec2-lora-kaa/final_model  (best epoch: 4, LoRA merged into the model)

TEST RESULTS  (final_model, greedy decoding, no language model)
  test set   clips      WER     CER
  STT test   1,950   38.43%   8.92%
  HF test      523   22.32%   3.65%
  Scores with beam search and the KenLM language model: see decode.log.
  decode.log gives 38.37% / 8.90% on STT test for this same model. The small difference is a flaw in this
  quick check: it also reads the padding after each clip, which added stray letters at the end of 125 of
  the 1,950 STT test clips (example: 'esaplanadıdı' instead of 'esaplanadı'). decode.py cuts the padding
  off, so the decode.log numbers are the right ones. HF test is the same in both.

